<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Covered California (Marketplace)</h2>
    <p class="lede">If your MAGI is above 138% FPL, Covered California is the primary path &mdash; federal premium tax credits for 100&ndash;400% FPL households, California state subsidies up to 200% FPL (2027), and reduced cost-sharing on Silver plans up to 250% FPL. Marketplace coverage has <strong>no work or hours test</strong>; eligibility runs on household income and access to other coverage. One critical exception: a person who <em>is</em> Medi-Cal-eligible but loses Medi-Cal for work-rule noncompliance cannot get Covered California financial help at all (full-cost plans only) &mdash; see CC-13 below.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="cc-h">
    <h3 id="cc-h">Rules and numbers</h3>
    {{TABLE:covered-california.csv::Covered California and ACA subsidy rules (2026-2027)}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h3 id="how-h">How the subsidies work</h3>
    <div class="panel blue">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Federal premium tax credit (PTC):</strong> available to households with income 100&ndash;400% FPL who buy through the marketplace and lack affordable other coverage. It caps what you pay for the benchmark Silver plan and can be taken in advance (lowering each month's bill) or at tax time. Because your income is rental + interest + trading gains rather than wages, nothing about the credit changes &mdash; MAGI is MAGI.</li>
        <li><strong>The 400% cliff is back:</strong> the enhanced credits that eliminated the cliff (and capped contributions) expired December 31, 2025 and were not extended by the One Big Beautiful Bill Act. Above 400% FPL ($63,840 for one person using the 2026 guidelines) there is no federal subsidy &mdash; a reason to watch realized trading gains late in the year.</li>
        <li><strong>California state subsidy:</strong> for 2027, $300 million was appropriated and eligibility extends to households up to 200% FPL ($31,920 individual / $66,000 family of four). Covered California projects 500,000+ recipients, with nearly 200,000 people able to choose a $0-premium Silver plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost-sharing reductions (CSR):</strong> if you pick a Silver plan and your income is 100&ndash;250% FPL, the plan variant is upgraded (Silver 94 / 87 / 73) with lower deductibles and out-of-pocket maximums. Above 250% and up to 400% FPL there is no reduction, because the statutory formula would push actuarial value past the 70% statutory ceiling (CMS 2026 payment-parameters guidance). For 2026 the reduced maximum annual limitation on cost sharing is $3,350 self-only / $6,700 other-than-self-only for the 94% and 87% variants, and $8,100 / $16,200 for the 73% variant.</li>
        <li><strong>Repay the difference:</strong> starting with tax year 2026, if your advance credit exceeded what your final income justified, the <em>entire</em> excess is repaid (P.L. 119-21 section 71305 removed the caps). Report income changes promptly &mdash; and note that capital gains realized in December can retroactively disqualify credits already taken.</li>
        <li><strong>No work requirement on the marketplace.</strong> Neither the federal premium tax credit nor Covered California applies any work, hours, or community-engagement test &mdash; this is an absence-based statement (the cited official pages list no such requirement); see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="status-h">
    <h3 id="status-h">Where the enhanced-credit extension stands</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>December 11, 2025:</strong> the Senate rejected cloture on the motion to proceed to S. 3385, 51&ndash;48, when three-fifths (60) was required &mdash; the three-year extension of the enhanced premium tax credits did not reach a vote (<a href="https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00644.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official roll-call record</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>January 8, 2026:</strong> H.R. 1834 passed the House 230&ndash;196 (Roll no. 11) and was received in the Senate (<a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1834" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Congress.gov</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>February 10, 2026:</strong> H.R. 1834 was read the second time and placed on the Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders (Calendar No. 319).</li>
        <li><strong>Status as of this snapshot:</strong> not law; the enhanced credits remain expired and the 400% FPL cliff is back.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="kbd-note">Flagged: H.R. 1834's official title (&ldquo;To advance policy priorities that will break the gridlock&rdquo;) does not describe the subsidy extension &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="when-h">
    <h3 id="when-h">When you can enroll</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ul class="timeline">
        <li><span class="when">October 1, 2026</span> Current members can start renewing or switching plans.</li>
        <li><span class="when">November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 31, 2027</span> Open enrollment for 2027 coverage. Covered California's Federal Changes guide states a June 12, 2026 ruling in <em>City of Columbus v. Kennedy</em> vacated the shortened annual open enrollment period for 2027, reverting California to the normal three-month period of November 1 through January 31. CMS states the <em>federally</em> facilitated exchange window is November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 15, 2027 &mdash; do not confuse the two.</li>
        <li><span class="when">December 31, 2026</span> Enroll by this date for coverage starting January 1, 2027.</li>
        <li><span class="when">January 31, 2027</span> Last day to enroll for coverage starting February 1, 2027.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="kbd-note">Sources: <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/enrollment/open-enrollment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California &mdash; Open Enrollment</a>; <a href="https://hbex.coveredca.com/toolkit/downloads/Federal_Changes_Guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California Federal Changes Quick Guide (June 19, 2026)</a>; <a href="https://www.cms.gov/files/document/statement-re-city-columbus-073126.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS statement on City of Columbus v. Kennedy</a>.</p>
      <p>Medi-Cal, by contrast, is open year-round &mdash; and losing Medi-Cal because income rose is itself a qualifying event for marketplace special enrollment. Losing it for the work rule is not the same thing: it removes marketplace subsidy eligibility entirely.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="penalty-h">
    <h3 id="penalty-h">The California penalty for staying uninsured</h3>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <p>California requires residents to have qualifying coverage, claim an exemption, or pay the Individual Shared Responsibility Penalty with their state return: at least <strong>$950 per adult</strong> (and $475 per dependent child) or <strong>2.5% of gross income over the filing threshold</strong>, whichever is more. Exemptions claimable on the state return include income below the tax filing threshold, unaffordable coverage, unaffordable family self-only coverage, coverage gaps of three months or less, health care sharing ministry membership, federally recognized tribe membership, incarceration, certain non-citizens, and enrollment in limited- or restricted-scope Medi-Cal. Since Medi-Cal and marketplace plans both count as coverage, the penalty mainly bites people who forgo coverage entirely.</p>
      <p class="kbd-note">Source: <a href="https://www.ftb.ca.gov/file/personal/filing-situations/health-care-mandate/personal.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FTB &mdash; Personal Health Care Mandate</a>. Flagged: the $950/$475 amounts are the figures FTB labels for the 2025 tax year and are indexed; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
